#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "89a4773c-f6bb-577d-8743-5d0a46c1da6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5573" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/assets/bc9289885d7d9db73bedf7486b311cf7333ea0cb72520ce3399a9b15f2c9b4c8.png)\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=1[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "4985be13-9b44-5ec5-b342-00354339b902" :question/math-academy-id "e-5574" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/assets/7e767fa1b102062adfe30a12660ee0854ecd14829cd158160e95c9efbcc718b5.png)\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3-2)}^{2}+{(2-2)}^{2}+{(1-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "82a0e170-d916-5ae7-bd72-9b47c7287e0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7173" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w},$ where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/assets/11a1a4da746f8286edde9e12f17a7eaae9f4a242dfdab8ed137674c2d8e0c04c.png) \nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane given that $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by   \n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\,\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{13\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{13\\cdot 5+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "7014f0ec-9ce3-598a-8ddb-b71a000d383f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7309" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf v,\\mathbf w\\}$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/assets/665de193fe6fda3cfc5b5ec38f61f2b69ccb0e5d72514d6622b0815c5c3f474b.png)\nFind the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.$ So, computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{10})}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{20}\\,\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{10\\,\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left( \\dfrac{{\\sqrt2}}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c1d0d4-da84-52bf-b93e-0c13066a06df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358770} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb6c99c-76a4-5925-9618-c8976a394310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358771} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdc04cf-3499-5f62-99f7-1da7b8ed286f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358772} {:answer/id #uuid "72912aa4-533e-502d-9af4-6deae7cce76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358773} {:answer/id #uuid "41dc1eec-6d8a-5357-8005-13576ffef99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41dc1eec-6d8a-5357-8005-13576ffef99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358774} :answer-field/id #uuid "d52d91bf-103b-5e2c-b620-5b7047927f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "835e6f56-4b55-54bc-8dba-c3d154485354" :question/math-academy-id "q-140367" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{6\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(6-4)}^{2}+{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(-4-(-2))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f1b3fc-6be1-512b-b0f0-ee87644a1650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309367} {:answer/id #uuid "d16d7909-b67c-5eb3-954f-655074d227cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309368} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6464a7-a537-5b6e-b252-b6091b1cf4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309369} {:answer/id #uuid "6733aca9-fc57-56c0-b3f3-b090ac5fe2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309370} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1dd863-5490-53e4-b835-2695e4378ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6733aca9-fc57-56c0-b3f3-b090ac5fe2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309370} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf703a9-7b21-5692-bcf1-5dd4d1095a89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fa47428-4db5-5247-a146-29b4cebc836f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39992" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set \n${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d29ebffe-7b4e-5d9f-bdba-5c1ecb74de9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131565} {:answer/id #uuid "18db8704-c3ae-55d9-aeec-2dc393a42bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131566} {:answer/id #uuid "305f7a56-e420-5e0a-aaa1-375007e53a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131567} {:answer/id #uuid "437131d3-209d-5440-8146-a7acea973eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131568} {:answer/id #uuid "6225371f-d2a8-508d-b6e3-8d38ebe91884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18db8704-c3ae-55d9-aeec-2dc393a42bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131566} :answer-field/id #uuid "d09a3b5d-acdb-524b-ab6f-c052153e160e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27fc7f62-58d5-5ef7-b202-bc3a925043e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40194" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(21[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fca5d6e2-7276-5b98-87b1-643396737379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358777} {:answer/id #uuid "28225b33-f11e-5c79-a815-ac4beb5347fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358778} {:answer/id #uuid "19dc3b36-981e-5f20-9ddb-773f2a8ebda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358779} {:answer/id #uuid "0a032b3a-9340-518f-ae4f-deb49ac9c02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358780} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc11ec3-bc8f-5041-a2a9-751d2d5c06f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fca5d6e2-7276-5b98-87b1-643396737379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358777} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee1d6a8b-afde-5aab-af3b-1c7dc52cd434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cec22da-e61e-52a7-b865-4b04e5b177ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-40218" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+9\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+9\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "456ea0a6-32b5-5da5-9939-2aeac5f40db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358784} {:answer/id #uuid "adbc3792-e676-5444-99d0-f82f1796a199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358785} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad88ab1-0e8e-5c9f-b5b5-93b706ad4cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358786} {:answer/id #uuid "f89a3ec3-9b17-5ea4-aad5-da0f9531eb5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358787} {:answer/id #uuid "f56efbf8-5a4e-5254-841c-79e27aadb84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f56efbf8-5a4e-5254-841c-79e27aadb84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358788} :answer-field/id #uuid "64aaf035-1ccf-532e-aa60-a9577abd171a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bac6bfb5-a42f-5950-aaa1-98a507c295d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40591" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The vector $x$ can be written as $x={a}_{1}-{a}_{2}.$ So, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ Therefore, the projection of $x$ onto $S$ is $x$ itself and the angle between $x$ and the subspace is equal to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba09dd52-71f4-5c94-8095-ce6179bcac47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309381} {:answer/id #uuid "112969a0-33d8-58a1-9802-8f2b6471ac9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309382} {:answer/id #uuid "41ed7f1c-9bfd-58cd-9dcc-d1e5f2a4a9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309383} {:answer/id #uuid "a80b0efd-b15a-52dc-93bb-c977e521ec06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309384} {:answer/id #uuid "85ca9509-047a-504b-9974-fed193ebd32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "112969a0-33d8-58a1-9802-8f2b6471ac9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309382} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce69b82-5134-572b-bb60-370523024825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b083e078-533c-51be-b620-d91910a453ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-40605" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^{2}+(0-0)^{2}+(-3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a90e3e1f-e050-52bd-990f-e3f26a1b1809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309395} {:answer/id #uuid "f75421ab-4941-5c0f-a101-8291a450cd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309396} {:answer/id #uuid "032a1e0b-f5a5-5232-9b50-912d7e3593f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309397} {:answer/id #uuid "6807ceac-729e-5bb7-8fe7-460e6a4b1089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309398} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4a357b-20b4-54bd-9acb-8502b534faae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a90e3e1f-e050-52bd-990f-e3f26a1b1809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309395} :answer-field/id #uuid "199b6905-551a-5894-85d9-e2418be61ed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3affa7c6-8db6-53c2-af21-9110c8fb933c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40615" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The fact that we got the zero-vector as the projection means that $x$ is orthogonal to the subspace $S.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfb16e53-12f3-57be-8f05-0eb43be26d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309402} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee825fb-2262-52ee-a613-729ebef83676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309403} {:answer/id #uuid "1d89d14d-ba40-5f49-a20e-8a35bc5ad9b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309404} {:answer/id #uuid "7672db66-6f55-5ab7-8cc3-c49cfdcfe04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309405} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd36e6d-6eb5-5d7e-8b06-181a5364f123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ee825fb-2262-52ee-a613-729ebef83676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309403} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc19ae9-e7f1-5aa1-bbcc-d9d381f71221" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afc5be3a-a134-55b4-b40c-0093014944dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-40616" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{20\\cdot 4+(-20)\\cdot 5+0\\cdot 0}{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{20\\cdot 5+(-20)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 0}{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{41}(-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answ