g the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7df928a2-bba2-5d88-824f-e306bc84b5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358819} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a3dbca-576f-5f5e-8f93-eb4b7ba22cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358820} {:answer/id #uuid "441abd51-c9f9-5c10-aaf5-0ea9b6fd30b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358821} {:answer/id #uuid "af7e7b01-e1d2-5c94-8a04-bd61ddad4d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358822} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3a43c7-e6f1-566b-b46c-fe61361e0531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3a3dbca-576f-5f5e-8f93-eb4b7ba22cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358820} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffe1b70b-1906-577f-9922-2782f4919914" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "141cccb3-1688-527f-8398-3c1abb65990a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81601" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 3+(-11)\\cdot (-4)+17\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 5+(-11)\\cdot 4+17\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4186be12-2e1c-5b44-8691-8b57f89175bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309444} {:answer/id #uuid "f420911b-16d3-5270-94ba-f8f7fbdd508a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309445} {:answer/id #uuid "36245817-c0b9-529e-a7f2-fb154a74b2b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309446} {:answer/id #uuid "7275dc04-817d-58cf-9ea2-db78f97b35d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309447} {:answer/id #uuid "06a21957-878b-5e5f-b0cd-588057cab18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06a21957-878b-5e5f-b0cd-588057cab18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309448} :answer-field/id #uuid "54da01ee-92bb-504f-a5c1-55e6114c3b34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "852e8788-cba4-5ef6-96d9-b4cd2873381e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82915" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-5)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ coincides with the projection, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is zero $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c3d900e-729e-5dec-bbd0-737c2b9aa3ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358826} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc9f3ea-1462-51df-9f38-5426df413617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358827} {:answer/id #uuid "f5653dfe-4837-5ff5-9133-bcd1a008b353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358828} {:answer/id #uuid "10af7bc7-932b-5785-a44a-2e121b933894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358829} {:answer/id #uuid "73d7fe21-207a-581c-a616-dfec4041ddf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c3d900e-729e-5dec-bbd0-737c2b9aa3ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358826} :answer-field/id #uuid "941b7fe8-b35d-57e7-9b93-d8aa8a066cc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d55179c8-8008-5c36-8302-ab011fa8de1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-82917" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 4+(-9)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}], \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 0+(-9)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-9)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 4}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-0)^{2}+(-3-(-5))^{2}+(-9-(-2))^{2}+(3-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{63} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2f792ae-b8e3-5c21-b8bc-334dafbcd4b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{12.11}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309451} {:answer/id #uuid "3b89cb54-e767-5c5d-a3b1-305b7020c9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25.24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309452} {:answer/id #uuid "0cdd4fb5-01e2-5e6b-ba98-a4c8714c3db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309453} {:answer/id #uuid "d668d40c-9a28-5184-8d6e-aaabf53684c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{11.12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309454} {:answer/id #uuid "98d71906-8c05-5a15-9ec9-e20a94433d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18.25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b89cb54-e767-5c5d-a3b1-305b7020c9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25.24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309452} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8bd3fd2-e289-5311-9140-b6f71db70b14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4417e099-8677-544d-a57a-1a6fa347aa4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82948" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{44}\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2\\sqrt{11}\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11})≈{25.24}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f808610-b7e8-56f4-af93-cc794fb8f85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309458} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7673a1-7656-52e5-b18e-02ccad4619f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309459} {:answer/id #uuid "940ef9bc-57fc-5858-a0d8-37f54a7c839c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309460} {:answer/id #uuid "afcf965c-e3e6-56a6-a1a5-556324362cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309461} {:answer/id #uuid "13ea00ec-9fae-5abe-a8a0-d727df109e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13ea00ec-9fae-5abe-a8a0-d727df109e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309462} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d6bea67-d5f4-5664-859b-bd9e4a8a8d97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d727048-e0d7-5a77-995e-bf03ee0a89fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-82950" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{5\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84231ab7-3deb-56ca-9b02-7524cf5f2438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309465} {:answer/id #uuid "a671535e-89de-550b-9fc8-7c0daf746afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48.59}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309466} {:answer/id #uuid "dedd7b54-2e2b-57f4-abb5-e501bfdd0fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{36.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309467} {:answer/id #uuid "5de29845-916b-50af-82aa-d5deed981db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29.91}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309468} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ec700-ce94-540c-9c34-3f1a5de9a83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7.18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84231ab7-3deb-56ca-9b02-7524cf5f2438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309465} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b989f56-c98e-5541-8090-aafb254c5786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a040e083-d81c-5acb-84bf-c293088be346" :question/math-academy-id "q-82978" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+7\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{\\sqrt{63}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})≈{28.13}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4b71f3-dc38-5620-9fce-87ea5ba4d627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358833} {:answer/id #uuid "997d7e10-f118-55ac-91f2-63df7be638f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358834} {:answer/id #uuid "b772fffd-95ff-5392-bd41-8a1fec375285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358835} {:answer/id #uuid "fac9ef11-18d8-5518-88ca-6914d3e41fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358836} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec2975e-fbdf-5ed0-85af-c9914aebcf94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b4b71f3-dc38-5620-9fce-87ea5ba4d627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358833} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b1d76bc-0f00-5a24-82fb-37502bab4aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3360a6d8-b9c1-5626-8aac-3aa15b49eddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-82979" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}]}
